    'Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy presents in the second half of pregnancy with pruritus, typically of palms and soles and worse at night, without a primary rash, and raised serum bile acids with or without raised transaminases; other causes of liver disease and itching are excluded. It is associated with preterm birth, meconium-stained fluid, fetal distress and stillbirth, with risk rising substantially when bile acids exceed about 100 µmol/L. Management includes monitoring bile acids and liver tests, ursodeoxycholic acid for symptoms, vitamin K if the prothrombin time is prolonged, and planned delivery timed by bile acid levels — often around 35–38 weeks. It resolves after delivery but recurs in later pregnancies.',
